				{
					"type": "MainSpriteFontEmoji1",
					"properties": {
						"text": "[color=005D36]Alex:[/color]\n\n[color=b7abbf]What do you see when you look at the compositions of yours that are highly detailed on a pixel by pixel scale, like '[color=ffffff]^[/color]Eeeeeeeee[color=ffffff]^[/color]' and 'Timed'? What ways of thinking might they encourage for you, and how does this differ from your more [color=9482a0]Stamp[/color]-heavy works and [color=9482a0]Spin[/color] pieces?[/color]\n\n[color=663E54]Wilsans:[/color]\n\n[color=b7abbf]I see effort, somehow. It takes a lot of time to get it all pixelated like that, because of all the [color=9482a0]Quicksands[/color] you have to do to jumble it all up...making it to the end and [color=9482a0]Mirroring[/color] it into a bunch of pretty patterns and making the colors all vibrant is definitely something that helps me push through.\n\nMy Spin pieces and less detailed Mirror works all started as experiments, while my pixel art has much more of a routine to it.\n\nBut they still all have one thing in common, the color. Eyestrain is my thing for No Paint, and I'd say I do a pretty good job at it.[/color]\n \n",
